 ## Subtopics
 
-### Region of Interest (ROI) Rendering
-A technique to visualize internal features by defining a region of interest and rendering only that portion of the dataset [S2, book]. It differs from standard cutting planes by allowing more complex region definitions (e.g., closed geometric objects, binary masks) rather than just a single half-space. For image-order ray casting, the ray is clipped against all geometric region definitions as a preprocessing step, and the ray function is evaluated only along segments within the ROI. Mask values are consulted at each sample to include or exclude contributions. For object-order methods, samples are checked against the ROI before incorporation. Hardware clipping planes can be used to support ROI [S2, book].
+### [Region of Interest (ROI) Rendering](10737-region-of-interest-roi-rendering.md)
+Region of Interest (ROI) rendering is a technique to visualize internal features by defining a specific region and rendering only that portion of the dataset. It solves the problem where full opacity is obtained before reaching internal structures, making them difficult to see. It sets itself apart from standard volume rendering by restricting the integration domain to a user-defined subset, which can be geometric or volumetric, rather than the entire volume extent.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels and the number of samples per ray within the visible region. The clipping operation adds a constant overhead per ray.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the volume size for storing the voxel data. The plane geometry and arrow add negligible memory overhead.
-*   **Performance:** Interactive performance is achievable because the clipping is simple and can be handled efficiently in the shader or as a preprocessing step. The source demonstrates real-time interaction with plane parameters [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, intuitive for users, allows immediate visual feedback of internal structures.
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to planar cuts. Cannot easily handle complex, non-planar regions of interest without more advanced techniques like ROI rendering [S2, book].
-*   **When to use:** When the user needs to quickly inspect internal structures by slicing the volume.
-*   **When not to use:** When complex, non-planar regions of interest are required, or when the volume is too large to fit in memory for real-time ray casting.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels and the number of samples per ray up to the cutting plane. The cutting plane reduces the average number of samples per ray compared to full volume rendering, potentially improving performance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the volume size for storing the voxel data. The plane mesh and arrow add negligible overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the resolution of the volume and the screen. The cutting plane can improve performance by reducing the integration length [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Interactive exploration of internal structures; simple implementation; reduces visual clutter from overlying opaque structures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Only slices along a single plane; may not be sufficient for complex internal structures that require multiple cuts or volumetric clipping [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When interactive exploration of internal structures is required, and a single planar cut is sufficient to reveal the desired features [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When the internal structures are complex and require multiple cuts or volumetric clipping to be fully visible [S1, unofficial].
 
